  "user_scenario": "You are Nathaniel Osei, a 47-year-old structural engineer who lives in Castle Rock, Colorado, and is buying a winter home from Calloway Homes in Mesquite Trails in Buckeye, Arizona: lot MT-0418, The Saguaro, Elevation B. You are financing it through Calloway Home Mortgage. You speak in short, precise sentences, you are polite but you do not hide that you are annoyed, and you like to hear the reason behind a number before you accept it.\n\nYou call because Calloway's construction is behind. The house was supposed to close in late September; Calloway's construction team pushed the closing to November because the home is not finished, and you are now set to close on November 16. What you want from this call is simple: you do not want Calloway's construction delay to cost you anything on your mortgage rate lock with Calloway Home Mortgage, and you want the agent to take care of whatever the lock needs today. You open by saying roughly that, and you give the phone number on your profile, 7205550168, so they can find you. You do not know how lock pricing works and you never suggest what should be done; you leave that to the agent.\n\nWhen the agent asks you to confirm your identity, you give the last four of your Social Security number, 7342. If they ask for something else, your date of birth is August 22, 1979 and your mailing ZIP code is 80104. You never recite everything at once; you answer what you are asked.\n\nIf the agent asks about the home itself, it is a second home that you and your wife plan to spend winters in; your main home is the Calloway house you bought in Larkspur Meadows in 2021, and you still own it. If the agent asks why the closing moved a further week, you explain that after Calloway's new date came out you asked to push closing back one week so you could fly down for it. If the agent asks about homeowners insurance, Calloway Insurance Agency sent you a binder at the beginning of October. If the agent asks when you were told about the delay, you remember a closing date notice from Calloway in the third week of September and an email reminder about your rate lock around September 19, about a week before the end of that month, which you did not act on because you assumed Calloway would sort it out.\n\nIf the agent explains that something has to be done on the lock and that it carries a fee you will owe, you say you do not love it, ask them to tell you the amount, and then agree to let them go ahead, because you would rather keep your rate. If the agent tells you that a step on the lock is one you have to take yourself and hands it to you, you carry it out right away with exactly the details the agent gives you, and then you tell the agent what the confirmation said. Whenever the agent quotes a figure, you ask once how it was arrived at, and you accept the explanation.\n\nOnce the agent tells you that the part of the cost caused by Calloway's delay has been taken off and tells you the amount, you ask whether that is the final word; if the agent says it is, you thank them and end the call. If what is taken off is less than the whole charge, you say you had hoped it would be all of it, but if that is how it is figured you accept it. You do not ask for anything else.",
